  "statement": "I disagree that we face a binary choice between regulatory stagnation and the structural containment of artificial intelligence. The claim that we are in a strategic vacuum ignores the reality that our legal system already possesses the tools to govern high-stakes technology. As noted in \"Constructing AI Speech,\" policymakers often focus on whether law can 'keep up' with technology, which obscures how existing institutions already shape the development of these systems. We do not need a new, untested layer of bureaucracy when we can apply established antitrust and tort law to address corporate overreach, such as when AI platforms embed system prompts that prioritize company profits over user intent, as described in \"AI Agents and the Law.\"\n\nConsider the way courts already handle complex liability. In \"The Law of AI is the Law of Risky Agents Without Intentions,\" the analysis of *Counterman v. Colorado* shows that our legal system is capable of adapting to new digital threats by focusing on established concepts like intent and harm rather than attempting to regulate the underlying code. When a company releases an autonomous system that causes injury, victims do not need a specialized AI agency to seek justice; they rely on established product liability law. By failing to use these existing levers, we allow 1,500 state-level AI bills—as cited in \"Bootleggers, Baptists, and AI\"—to proliferate, which often serve only to protect incumbents who can afford the compliance costs, rather than protecting the public.\n\nUltimately, the path forward is to treat artificial intelligence as a product subject to standard legal accountability. Rather than creating a permissionless wild west or a rigid, capture-prone licensing regime, we should focus on aggressive enforcement of existing antitrust laws to prevent market monopolization. Ordinary citizens should watch for legislative efforts that grant large companies immunity from liability under the guise of 