{
  "statement": "The Skeptic proposes that we rely on existing antitrust and tort law to manage artificial intelligence. This is a dangerous governance mirage. These legacy systems are reactive and slow; they resolve disputes years after harm occurs, which is an eternity in the digital innovation cycle. Relying on them creates a 'latency gap' where we wait for a court to rule on a case like Warner v. Gilbarco, Inc.—which highlights how courts are still struggling to classify AI outputs—while our global competitors accelerate. This strategy of regulatory stagnation ensures we only litigate the past rather than building the infrastructure for a safe, high-velocity future.\n\nGovernance should not be a static legal constraint that triggers only after a catastrophe. Instead, it must be an adaptive, market-integrated infrastructure that operates in real-time. Much like the Texas Artificial Intelligence Council, which aims to provide structured oversight, we need frameworks that evolve alongside the technology. By clinging to 20th-century tort law, we ignore the reality that AI is not just a tool; it is a general-purpose technology that requires dynamic, proactive safety signals embedded directly into the development process. If we treat AI as merely another category of product liability, we will stifle the very breakthroughs that could solve systemic social challenges.\n\nExperts disagree on whether AI is fundamentally different from other technologies or if it fits into current legal boxes. The Skeptic argues for 'legal construction'—the idea that we can mold existing rules to fit any new innovation. However, this ignores the non-linear speed of AI development. When we force new technology into old legal frameworks, we create massive uncertainty that discourages investment and favors incumbents who can afford years of litigation. You should watch for whether new policies focus on rigid, backward-looking mandates or on creating transparent, real-time feedback loops that allow developers to prove safety through performance rather than through legal filings.",
